<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1970</h1><p class="ref-lede">1970 in world history: 15 events, 3 births, 19 deaths, 1 work, 2 states founded and 4 that came to an end. 232 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1966">1966</a><a href="/years/1967">1967</a><a href="/years/1968">1968</a><a href="/years/1969">1969</a><a href="/years/1970" aria-current="page">1970</a><a href="/years/1971">1971</a><a href="/years/1972">1972</a><a href="/years/1973">1973</a><a href="/years/1974">1974</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Unix epoch: the zero point of computer time</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Midnight UTC on this date was later chosen as the reference point from which Unix and its descendants count time in seconds. The convention still underpins timekeeping on most of the world's computers and phones.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-12">12 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Biafra announces its surrender, ending the Nigerian Civil War</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Biafra's acting leader Philip Effiong announced the end of the secessionist state's resistance. Between half a million and two million people had died, many from famine caused by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-5">5 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty enters into force</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The treaty committed nuclear powers not to spread weapons and others not to acquire them, while promoting peaceful nuclear energy. With 191 parties, it is the most widely joined arms control agreement.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Apollo 13 lifts off for the Moon</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> 13 launched from Kennedy Space Center. Two days out an oxygen tank exploded, the lunar landing was abandoned, and the crew used the lunar module as a lifeboat to return safely.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-22">22 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Millions mark the first Earth Day</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Some 20 million Americans joined rallies and teach-ins for the first Earth Day. Its momentum helped create the Environmental Protection Agency later that year.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">National Guard kills four students at Kent State</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> National Guardsmen fired on students protesting the US invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, killing four and wounding nine. The shootings set off a nationwide student strike.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-31">31 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Earthquake and avalanche devastate northern Peru</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A magnitude 7.9 earthquake set off an avalanche from Huascarán that buried the town of Yungay. About 70,000 people died, making it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s worst natural disaster.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-18">18 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Jimi Hendrix dies in London aged 27</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Guitarist Jimi Hendrix died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> after taking barbiturates. In four years of fame he had transformed the electric guitar with his use of feedback, distortion and virtuoso technique.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-12">12 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Bhola cyclone devastates East Pakistan</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A cyclone and storm surge struck the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>, killing perhaps 300,000 to 500,000 people. Anger at the government's response fed the movement that led to Bangladesh's independence.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-17">17 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Luna 17 lands Lunokhod 1 on the Moon</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Soviet Luna 17 landed in the Sea of Rains and released Lunokhod 1, the first remote-controlled rover on another world. Driven from Earth, it travelled about 10 kilometres over ten months.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/koza-riot" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koza-riot.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolt</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/koza-riot">Koza riot</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Koza riot was an outbreak of violence in the city of Koza, now Okinawa City, on the night of 20 December 1970, during the American administration of Okinawa.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolution</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/black-power-revolution">Black Power Revolution</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Black Power Revolution was a wave of protests in Trinidad and Tobago in February–April 1970, led by students and the National Joint Action Committee, demanding economic power for Afro- and Indo-Trinidadians.</p></div></li></ol></section>
